  assign comp_pitches = 'For 25 years, teachers across Northern Tasmania have sent in student art. Selected pieces are hung, professionally presented, in a real museum gallery.|Frog conservation through art. Open to all ages and free to enter, so Aussie kids are welcome. Quirky, niche and surprisingly popular.|Run an investigation from question to final numbers and share it on an e-poster. Years 3 to 12, cash prizes per division. The organisers keep all entries.|The youth arm of Flickerfest, Australia’s Academy Award qualifying short film festival. Winning films screen at Bondi beside the international lineup.|Anyone under 18 in Australia makes original art on the year’s children’s rights theme. The winning piece becomes the 2027 national Children’s Week poster.|In its 21st year. Gladstone region primary kids make art about the Australia they care about, and chosen pieces travel to sister city Saiki in Japan.|Kids make a painting, drawing, print or photo about saying goodbye, remembering someone, or the people who care for us. Two student awards of $500 each.|A small US festival comp that now draws entries from dozens of countries. A perfect home for kids who love owls. Original art only, posted to Minnesota.|Going since 1924. The ACA picks a winner from the cartoons kids send in to theINmag each issue. The winner gets a year of Inkspot, their own magazine.|Crayola’s year-round open call. Less about winning, more about joining a global creative movement. Kids share what they make, tagged #StayCreative.|Not a competition, a challenge. Read books from your state’s list and earn a certificate signed by the Premier. A big motivator for reluctant readers.|Your drawing, story or comic on a real printed page, in the hands of thousands of kids across Australia. Send in anything you’ve made, any time. Send it IN.|Australia’s biggest creativity showcase, running since 1869. Hundreds of junior classes, and winning entries hang in the Arts & Crafts Pavilion.|Australia’s coolest under-the-radar film festival. Selected films premiere in Hobart, then tour nationally. Phone footage is fine.|Design your own A3 poster on the year’s Harmony Day theme, Foundation to Year 12. $150 for each year-group winner, $250 for the overall winner per section.|Australia’s oldest and biggest maths comp, entered through schools since 1978. Every student gets an award, and the Olympiad pathway starts here.|For high-achieving students who love a challenge! In-class problem solving competitions comprising 4 contests, 5 questions each and no calculators allowed.|Run by Children’s Rights Queensland with the Queensland Family and Child Commission. Age-group winners shared over $2,000 in prizes in 2025.|Where kids fall in love with engineering. Teams build a LEGO robot to solve missions, then compete from regional level all the way to international.|Run by the NSW Advocate for Children and Young People, with themes built around children’s rights. Group entries are welcome, three age categories.|Shoot a film on a phone or tablet, up to ten minutes, and the finalists screen at the Sydney Opera House. Kids and teens anywhere in the world can enter.|Kids draw a Christmas card front cover and the winning design is printed on actual Smiggle Christmas cards. Prizes usually include vouchers and Smiggle gear.|ACT kids, preschool to Year 6, make a poster urging one sustainability action, like saving water. Winners by age group plus a supported artwork category.|300,000+ students have told a story through dance, drama and music at Australia’s national story-dance festival, live in professional theatres or on video.|Run by Marion City Council’s library network. First prize up to $200, second up to $100. Word limits 300-750 (8-10s) or 500-1,000 (11-13s). Open theme.|South Australian kids, Reception to Year 12, enter a model, invention, game or app in awards run since 1981. Cash prizes up to $500; the entry stays yours.|Your design could become a Spencil product, co-designed with the Aussie stationery brand. Winners get $100 cash and $200 of Spencil gear. Ages 13 and under.|The little sibling of the Archibald. Kids paint or draw a portrait of someone who matters to them, and finalists hang in the Art Gallery of NSW.|Run by ANSTO, Australia’s nuclear science organisation. Free to enter, with generous prizes for winning schools. Particularly strong at primary level.|The Whitlam Institute asks kids one question, what matters? Answer however you want. Top prize is $1,500 and every entry is read by a real human.|A regional WA gem. Kids get a 30x30cm canvas to paint, and finalists hang alongside the famous Cossack Art Awards. The overall winner takes home $300.|One of Australia’s longest-running kids’ poetry comps, named for the writer of My Country. Cash prizes, plus an annual anthology of the best poems.|Goulburn Valley kids write a short story or a poem on any subject. First prize in each category is $300, judging is blind, and the author keeps copyright.|The classic spoken spelling bee for NSW Years 3 to 6. Kids progress from class to school to regionals, with State Finals held at Penrith.|Run by the NSW Department of Education with the State Library. Winning stories are published in the Best of WriteOn anthology, kids’ words in print.|Since 1952, Victorian kids have entered a picture book or an invention in this science comp. Prep to Year 12, tens of thousands in bursaries, all sections.|Run by Perth’s kids’ lit festival, Scribblers. The overall winner takes home an e-reader and book pack, and shortlisted writers get book subscriptions.|Run by the CYA Conference for children’s and young adult writers. Cash prizes of $100, $60 and $40, with categories across different writing forms.|Victorian kids aged 5 to 18 can enter one story and one poem each. 14 winners share QBD Books vouchers and are celebrated at an awards night.|Ages 5 to 12. Photograph Sydney on any camera, even a phone. Up to 20 photos hang at Customs House for a year, and the top prize is a $2,199 camera pack.|Write a nature poem of 20 lines or fewer and a tree gets planted for it. Shortlisted and winning poems go into a digital library.|Live or study in WA’s Midwest? Writers in Years 3 to 12 can win cash for prose and poetry, and a perpetual trophy goes on display at the winner’s school.|Named after picture book legend Shaun Tan. Winning art hangs for a month at Subiaco Library, professionally framed, with the City picking up the tab.|Cash prizes for first, second and third in every age category, Grades 3 to 12. Any subject at all, with word limits that step up by year level.|Run by Boroondara Library Service in Melbourne since 1999. The young writers categories share over $5,000 in prize money, huge for a council award.|A small-but-mighty comp from the Rocky River Writers Club in country SA, with prose and poetry streams. Note: entries must be posted, not emailed.|ACT school kids draw an A4 poster on the year’s Reconciliation Day theme. The winning school takes $1,000 and the poster can become a public mural.|Australia’s largest junior art, craft and design display. Ages 2 to 20, over 250 classes from painting to photography, and entries go on show at the Show.|Shortlisted writers score a one-on-one workshop with a published local author, and finalists are published in the SPYWA Anthology. Prizes up to $150.|Aussie kids’ clothing brand Banabae prints the winner’s art onto real clothes in their next collection. The winner also takes home a $500 voucher.|Make a real book, cover to cover: original story, original illustrations, hand-bound, solo or in a duo. Run by the Children’s Book Council of Australia (WA).|Australia Zoo’s photo comp. Kids category winners get $1,000 cash plus two nights at Crocodile Hunter Lodge, worth over $1,800. Three age groups up to 17.|A five-day online maths comp where participation comes first. Top schools win up to $1,500 and every student gets a certificate.|Entered through NSW schools. Fifty pieces of student art are gifted to The Children’s Hospital at Westmead every single year.|NT kids draw, paint, sculpt or shoot a photo for the Royal Darwin Show, which turned 75 in 2026. Champion Junior Art wins $50 and a rosette.|Run by the Australian Conservation Foundation. Kids make art about an Aussie threatened species, and judges look for heart over technical skill.|Prep to Year 6 kids get a division all their own. Queensland teams across 13 regions take seven weeks on a big challenge with zero outside help.|A national spelling tradition for Years 3 to 8. Kids compete from school to a national final, and past winners have met the actual Prime Minister.|Teams get six weeks on one big problem with zero adult help, then perform their solution to a live audience and face a surprise challenge on the day.|Founded by SAP and free to enter, with about $30,000 in cash prizes each year. Kids build real tech projects, from coding apps to building robots.|Run by Sydney bookstore Better Read Than Dead. Every entry is published in an anthology, so just by entering, kids become published writers.|NT kids write and make a real book, cover to cover. The Young Territory Author of the Year takes $500, and entries join a Darwin library collection.|A state-by-state comp for young composers, from solo instrument to rock, pop and EDM. Winning compositions are performed live at the awards ceremony.|A nice low-key one for upper primary writers. $100 prize plus the winning story published on the Book Links website. Open theme.|Draw a design on the theme Art in Bloom. Annette Blair blows the winning design in glass live at Floriade, and the finished piece goes home with the kid.|Not just Catholic schools: open to every Australian school for art on caring for creation. Vouchers up to $300, plus a $300 People’s Choice by public vote.|Run by Victorian water corporations. Kids design a poster on the year’s water theme, and category winners progress to state finals in National Water Week.|For Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander kids across South Australia, preschool to Year 12. Art on the year’s NAIDOC theme is exhibited at the SA Museum.|Tasmanian kids aged 8 to 19 enter art, sculpture or photos for the Royal Hobart Show youth exhibition at Hobart City Hall. Placegetters win up to $50.|Tasmanian kids, Kinder to Year 12, tackle the theme Seeds of Science as a picture book, photo essay, video or invention. Own division for Kinder to Year 2.|NT kids build a bug from pre-used materials and explain where it lives, or make a Crocwise poster. Top 3 in every category at every year level win cash.|Nearly $20,000 in prizes, all paid towards musical equipment or tuition. A breakthrough comp for any kid who writes songs in their bedroom.|Every entrant gets feedback and selected pieces hang in a real Footscray exhibition. Victorian kids 8 to 16 can enter art on paper, canvas or sculpture.|One original poem, any Australian school kid. Winner takes $1,000 and their poem joins performances seen by over 100,000 students. Poet keeps copyright.|Queensland kids from Prep to Year 6 make art, sculpture and poems for set classes, and entries are prepared for display at the Ekka in August.|The comp Robert Irwin won as a kid. Run by the SA Museum with Australian Geographic. Junior winners take home $500 and every finalist gets exhibited.|Grab a free colouring sheet at any Eckersley’s store or online, colour it in however you like, and winners take home $50 Eckersley’s gift cards.|Faber-Castell and Cool Australia set a classroom brief each year. The school behind the most inspiring entries scores $1,500 of Faber-Castell products.|Aussie-owned art supplies brand Micador runs regular giveaways like Win a Year of Micador, with ranges for toddlers right through to tweens.' | split: '|'
